          "case_name": "DeShaney v. Winnebago County Department of Social Services",
          "citation": "489 U.S. 189 (1989)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1989,
          "date_decided": "1989-02-22",
          "holding": "The Court held that the Due Process Clause imposes no affirmative duty on a state to protect a child from private violence, so social workers who received abuse reports and failed to remove the child did not deprive him of liberty by their inaction.",
          "relevance": "The controlling authority limiting constitutional liability for a decision to screen out or close a report.",
          "quote": "nothing in the language of the Due Process Clause itself requires the State to protect the life, liberty, and property of its citizens against invasion by private actors",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112202/deshaney-v-winnebago-county-department-of-social-services/",
